    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d495">

                <pb facs="0553" n="541" xml:id="pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M) Bolivia/52: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Bolivia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dawson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-01-22T15:00:00-04:00">January 22, 1942—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:33 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>31. Department’s 26, January 21, 6 p.m. Either first or second plan seems
                    satisfactory if modified by agreement with British for them to keep out of
                    Bolivian market competitively; second appears to Legation to be preferable. It
                    is obviously as much to their interest as to ours for prices to be held down to
                    a reasonable level. If Metals Reserve were the only authorized purchaser for the
                    United Nations all Bolivian antimony should flow to the United States except
                    perhaps for very small amounts to Argentina and we could keep our commitments to
                    the British by turning over to them amount agreement [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >agreed</hi>] upon. A less satisfactory solution would be for British to
                    establish ceiling prices equivalent to ours.</p>
                <p>Department’s desire for reasonable bilateral agreement is shared by Legation but
                    unfortunately not be [<hi rendition="#italic">by</hi>] Hochschild or Banco
                    Minero. Crux of matter is that antimony comes almost entirely from small
                    producers and that Hochschild and Banco Minero want to make utmost possible
                    profit as middlemen.</p>
                <p>Inslee,<anchor n="53" xml:id="d495-f1"/> Bohan<anchor n="54" xml:id="d495-f2"/>
                    and Worcester<anchor n="55" xml:id="d495-f3"/> are absent but from earlier
                    conversations I feel that they would concur in the above. Pixley<anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d495-f4"/> and Oberbillig<anchor n="57" xml:id="d495-f5"/> do.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d495-f1" n="53" xml:id="d495-f1-content">
                <p>Joseph A. Inslee, member of the United States Economic Mission to Bolivia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d495-f2" n="54" xml:id="d495-f2-content">
                <p>Merwin L. Bohan, Chief of the United States Economic Mission.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d495-f3" n="55" xml:id="d495-f3-content">
                <p>John Worcester, attached to the Economic Mission.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d495-f4" n="56" xml:id="d495-f4-content">
                <p>Rex A. Pixley, Assistant Chief of the Economic Mission.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d495-f5" n="57" xml:id="d495-f5-content">
                <p>Presumably Ernest E. Oberbillig, official of the Board of Economic Warfare.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
